window.__WIKI_INDEX__ = [{"u":"index.html","p":"Overview","t":"Hermes Agent — Engineering Wiki","a":"","x":"Hermes Agent is a self-improving, multi-platform AI agent harness from Nous Research (MIT-licensed) that drives a single user message to completion through one provider-agnostic turn loop, calls real tools over an RPC stub so a fifty-step pipeline costs one inference turn, and runs against roughly three dozen inference providers — including subscription-OAuth backends like ChatGPT Plus and Claude Pro/Max — while clinging to the two things that actually matter in production: the model's context window and its hourly rate quota."},{"u":"index.html","p":"Overview","t":"What Hermes is","a":"what-hermes-is","x":"What Hermes is Hermes is the agent runtime that wraps a chat model in everything it needs to act in the world: a terminal, a filesystem, web search, code execution, memory, and a gateway onto messaging platforms. It is provider-agnostic by design. The same production turn loop, AIAgent.run_conversation() at run_agent.py:5144 (a thin forwarder into agent.conversation_loop.run_conversation ), is used identically by the CLI and the gateway, and it speaks to every backend through the OpenAI ChatCompletion spec plus an Anthropic Messages transport, with adapters for Codex Responses and Bedrock Converse. Provider differences are quarantined inside _build_api_kwargs and per-provider adapter modules so the loop body stays uniform. That loop is deliberately simple at its core — a synchronous, single-threaded while (api_call_count < agent.max_iterations and agent.iteration_budget.remaining > 0) at agent/conversation_loop.py:461 — and deliberately paranoid at its edges. Each iteration assembles per-call api_messages (an ephemeral system prompt plus memory and plugin injections plus KV-cache breakpoints), calls the model through a streaming-first interruptible transport, normalizes whatever the provider returns into a uniform OpenAI-style SimpleNamespace , and branches on tool-calls-versus-text. Tool calls dispatch through _execute_tool_calls() at run_agent.py:5043 — which routes to execut"},{"u":"index.html","p":"Overview","t":"What's in this wiki","a":"whats-in-this-wiki","x":"What's in this wiki This wiki is organized into focused reference pages. Start wherever your question lives: Architecture The production turn loop end to end — message assembly, the streaming-first transport, response normalization into a uniform SimpleNamespace , tool dispatch via _execute_tool_calls() , the concurrent-versus-sequential paths with path-overlap checks, and how the single run_conversation loop in agent/conversation_loop.py is shared by the CLI and the gateway. Read the loop → Self-Improvement The closed learning loop: trajectory compression, cron-scheduled routines, the iteration/budget model (including the refund for programmatic turns), and the auxiliary-LLM guardian inside the approval gate. Read the loop → Internals The clever bits: Programmatic Tool Calling ( execute_code ) over an RPC stub, the structured context compactor with content-hash dedup and tool-pair repair, serverless filesystem-snapshot persistence on Modal/Daytona, and the stateful-remote-shell trick. Read the internals → Providers & Auth The PROVIDER_REGISTRY of ~34 providers, the six auth_type values, subscription-OAuth flows (Nous device code, Codex/ChatGPT PKCE, GitHub Copilot, Claude Pro/Max), the cross-process credential pool, and the on-disk auth store. Read the registry → FAQ Quick answers to the questions that recur: which providers need a subscription, why streaming is always on, wha"},{"u":"index.html","p":"Overview","t":"Highlights at a glance","a":"highlights","x":"Highlights at a glance The recurring theme across Hermes is that the non-obvious win is almost always about protecting the LLM's context window, surviving real-world rate limits, or making stateless cloud backends behave like stateful shells. Capability What it does Where Closed learning loop Self-improving agent: trajectory compression feeds experience back, cron routines run unattended, and a guardian LLM gates approvals. tools/approval.py:991 RPC zero-context tool calls execute_code : the LLM writes a Python script that calls real Hermes tools over an RPC stub, so a 50-call pipeline costs one inference turn and intermediate outputs never enter context. Internals Six terminal backends Local plus serverless (Modal/Daytona) sandboxes that filesystem-snapshot on cleanup and restore next session to fake persistence on hibernating sandboxes. Internals Context compression The live ContextCompressor dedups identical tool results by MD5 content hash, replaces old outputs with typed information-bearing one-liners (not dumb placeholders), and repairs orphaned tool_call/result pairs so the provider never 400s. ( trajectory_compressor.py is the separate offline compressor for training-data trajectories.) agent/context_compressor.py:907 Multi-platform gateway One agent fronts messaging platforms through the same shared run_conversation the CLI uses — no second code path. run_agent.py:5144"},{"u":"index.html","p":"Overview","t":"How to read the codebase","a":"reading-map","x":"How to read the codebase If you are reading Hermes for the first time, walk these files in order — they form a spine from the turn loop out to the providers. File Why it matters run_agent.py The AIAgent class and its public surface (~5.4k lines). It exposes run_conversation() at run_agent.py:5144 and _execute_tool_calls() at run_agent.py:5043 as thin forwarders into the agent/ package, where the loop body now lives. agent/conversation_loop.py The production turn loop itself: run_conversation() at agent/conversation_loop.py:371 , the budget while loop at agent/conversation_loop.py:461 , the cache-friendly sort_keys re-serialization at agent/conversation_loop.py:728 , the always-streaming decision at agent/conversation_loop.py:958 , and the empty/degenerate-response recovery (retry → fallback provider) around agent/conversation_loop.py:3994 . Concurrent vs. sequential tool dispatch lives in agent/tool_executor.py:243 , and the path-overlap parallelism check is _paths_overlap at agent/tool_dispatch_helpers.py:166 . tools/approval.py The approval gate and the embedded auxiliary-LLM guardian _smart_approve at tools/approval.py:991 , gated in by the Smart-approval phase at tools/approval.py:1378 . agent/context_compressor.py Live context compaction: MD5 content-hash dedup at agent/context_compressor.py:907 , information-bearing one-liner replacement, and _sanitize_tool_pairs orphan r"},{"u":"architecture.html","p":"Architecture","t":"Architecture: The Loops","a":"","x":"Hermes is built around a single, synchronous turn loop — AIAgent.run_conversation() at run_agent.py:5144 , a thin forwarder into the extracted loop body at agent/conversation_loop.py:371 — that drives one user message to completion. Almost everything else in the system is a driver wrapped around that loop: the CLI REPL, the multi-platform gateway, the cron/routines scheduler, the parallel batch runner, and the recursive delegation of subagents. After the upstream merge removed the Atropos RL/eval environments, there is effectively one production loop plus its drivers — no second async loop survives. This page dissects the main loop and its tool pipeline first, then maps each driver onto it and contrasts them in a single table."},{"u":"architecture.html","p":"Architecture","t":"Overview: One Loop, Many Drivers","a":"overview","x":"Overview: One Loop, Many Drivers The turn loop is AIAgent.run_conversation() — a public forwarder at run_agent.py:5144 whose body now lives in agent/conversation_loop.py:run_conversation ( agent/conversation_loop.py:371 ). It is a synchronous function that returns a result dict containing final_response , messages , api_calls , completed , and token usage; that dict is assembled by the extracted finalize_turn helper ( agent/turn_finalizer.py:326 ). Every driver in the system ultimately calls this exact function. Key insight. The framing of \"lighter CLI loop vs heavier gateway loop\" is misleading. The inner loop is byte-identical run_conversation in every case — drivers differ only at the outer level (agent lifecycle, callback wiring, approval transport). The upstream merge (commit 5af672c75) deleted the Atropos RL/eval environments, so the old \"second async HermesAgentLoop \" is gone: there is one production loop, period. The drivers fan out like this — all roads lead back to run_conversation :"},{"u":"architecture.html","p":"Architecture","t":"The Main Agent Loop","a":"main-loop","x":"The Main Agent Loop Per-turn preamble Before entering the loop, run_conversation delegates substantial per-turn setup to the extracted build_turn_context helper ( agent/turn_context.py:64 , called from agent/conversation_loop.py:407 ). That helper: Sanitizes surrogate characters in the user message — lone surrogates crash json.dumps() inside the OpenAI SDK — via _sanitize_surrogates ( agent/turn_context.py:114 , agent/message_sanitization.py:31 ). Resets a wall of per-turn retry counters — _invalid_tool_retries , _empty_content_retries , _thinking_prefill_retries , and others ( agent/turn_context.py:132 ). Builds or loads the system prompt once per session for prefix-cache stability: only when _cached_system_prompt is unset does it call restore_or_build_system_prompt , so continuing sessions replay the stored prompt and keep the Anthropic cache prefix bit-identical ( agent/turn_context.py:232 ). Runs preflight compression if the loaded history already exceeds the threshold, looping up to 3 passes ( agent/turn_context.py:294 ). Fires plugin pre_llm_call hooks whose returned context is injected into the user message — never the system prompt, to preserve the cache prefix ( agent/turn_context.py:316 ). Creates a fresh IterationBudget(agent.max_iterations) per call ( agent/turn_context.py:164 ) and prefetches external memory once ( agent/turn_context.py:367 ). The user message is a"},{"u":"architecture.html","p":"Architecture","t":"The Tool-Execution Pipeline","a":"tool-pipeline","x":"The Tool-Execution Pipeline Registration: how tools appear Tools self-register at import time. Each module calls registry.register(name, toolset, schema, handler, ...) ( tools/registry.py:234 ), which populates a thread-safe ToolEntry map ( tools/registry.py:77 ) and rejects shadowing of built-ins (only MCP-over-MCP overwrites are allowed, tools/registry.py:260 ). The agent builds agent.tools once via get_tool_definitions(enabled_toolsets, disabled_toolsets, quiet_mode) and derives agent.valid_tool_names — the set the loop validates every model tool call against ( agent/agent_init.py:950 , agent/agent_init.py:959 ). get_tool_definitions ( model_tools.py:272 ) resolves the requested toolsets to a name set, asks the registry only for tools whose check_fn passes (so missing API keys hide tools, model_tools.py:413 ), then dynamically rewrites schemas : execute_code 's sandbox-tool list ( model_tools.py:422 ) and browser_navigate 's description ( model_tools.py:464 ) are pruned to actually-available tools so the model cannot hallucinate absent tools. The four _AGENT_LOOP_TOOLS = {todo, memory, session_search, delegate_task} are short-circuited ( model_tools.py:569 ) — they must be handled inside the loop with agent state, so a slip through to handle_function_call returns a stub error ( model_tools.py:1018 ). Note. toolset_distributions.py is orthogonal to the live loop: its DISTRIBU"},{"u":"architecture.html","p":"Architecture","t":"Provider Transport & Normalization","a":"transport","x":"Provider Transport & Normalization agent.api_mode is resolved once at agent init to one of chat_completions , anthropic_messages , codex_responses , bedrock_converse , or codex_app_server from explicit config, provider name, or base-URL heuristics ( agent/agent_init.py:314 ). The merge extracted the per-provider divergence into a ProviderTransport hierarchy under agent/transports/ — the agent fetches the active one via _get_transport() ( run_agent.py:4391 ) and the loop body calls only its uniform validate_response / map_finish_reason / normalize_response methods. Divergence is concentrated in two places: Request build — _build_api_kwargs forwards to build_api_kwargs ( agent/chat_completion_helpers.py:555 ): anthropic_messages delegates to build_anthropic_kwargs ( agent/anthropic_adapter.py:2253 ) plus convert_messages_to_anthropic ( agent/anthropic_adapter.py:2189 ); bedrock_converse builds a Converse payload; codex_responses builds a Responses-API payload ( input , instructions , reasoning.effort , prompt_cache_key ); otherwise a plain chat-completions dict. Transport call — _interruptible_api_call forwards to interruptible_api_call ( agent/chat_completion_helpers.py:125 ), dispatching to Codex stream, client.messages.create , boto3 converse , or client.chat.completions.create . The streaming variant interruptible_streaming_api_call ( agent/chat_completion_helpers.py:1567 ) m"},{"u":"architecture.html","p":"Architecture","t":"Context-Window Management","a":"context","x":"Context-Window Management The loop holds one ContextCompressor as agent.context_compressor , a ContextEngine subclass ( agent/context_engine.py:32 , agent/context_compressor.py:563 ). It exposes only two loop touchpoints: Preflight (before the loop, in build_turn_context ): if the loaded history estimate meets the threshold, compress up to 3 passes, resetting retry counters and clearing conversation_history so all compressed messages flush to a new session ( agent/turn_context.py:294 ). Per tool-turn (inside the loop): after _execute_tool_calls , should_compress(_real_tokens) is checked using the real last_prompt_tokens reported by the API (rough estimate fallback when usage is zero), and _compress_context rewrites messages + active_system_prompt when it fires ( agent/conversation_loop.py:3812 ). Initialization sets threshold_tokens = max(context_length * threshold_percent, MINIMUM_CONTEXT_LENGTH) with protect_first_n=3 and protect_last_n=20 defaults ( agent/context_compressor.py:629 , agent/context_compressor.py:645 ). should_compress simply compares tokens to threshold_tokens ( agent/context_compressor.py:785 ); usage is fed via update_from_response ( agent/context_compressor.py:741 ). The deep summarization mechanics live in context_compressor.compress ( agent/context_compressor.py:1984 ) and trajectory_compressor.py , but from the loop's perspective compression is just two "},{"u":"architecture.html","p":"Architecture","t":"The Drivers","a":"other-loops","x":"The Drivers Sourcing note. The details below were reconstructed directly from the codebase (cron, gateway, batch_runner, delegate_tool) and the verified main-loop findings. The Atropos RL/eval environment driver ( environments/agent_loop.py , HermesAgentLoop ) was deleted by upstream commit 5af672c75 and no longer exists, so it is not listed here. Citations point at the live source. Gateway loop (driver, not a new loop) The gateway is a long-lived process managing all configured platform adapters. Per inbound message it resolves a per-turn route, then reuses a session-keyed cached AIAgent when the config signature matches (preserving the frozen system prompt and tool schemas for cache hits), else builds a fresh one ( gateway/run.py:13999 , signature at gateway/run.py:12223 ). It rebinds callbacks ( tool_progress_callback , step_callback , stream_delta_callback , status_callback , gateway/run.py:14068 ) and calls agent.run_conversation(...) via the async _run_agent() ( gateway/run.py:13034 , call site at gateway/run.py:10119 ). Approval flips from synchronous input() to an async /approve transport. So the gateway is one outer driver around the same inner loop, differing in agent lifecycle (session cache vs CLI's singleton, whose turn runs at cli.py:10192 ), callback wiring, and approval transport. Cron / routines loop This is a genuinely separate outer loop, but it still drives "},{"u":"architecture.html","p":"Architecture","t":"Per-Driver Comparison","a":"comparison","x":"Per-Driver Comparison Driver Entry point Sync / async Concurrency model Drives run_conversation? Budget / turn cap Memory + plugins + compression CLI cli.py:10192 Sync (daemon thread + interrupt queue) One long-lived singleton agent Yes IterationBudget Yes Gateway gateway/run.py:13034 Sync turn on async-served message Per-session cached agent Yes IterationBudget Yes Cron / routines cron/scheduler.py:1306 Sync job on a ticker thread File-locked tick; 1-worker pool per job Yes IterationBudget + inactivity timeout Partial (headless, some toolsets disabled) Batch (datagen) batch_runner.py:349 Sync per prompt Multiprocessing Pool over batches Yes IterationBudget No ( skip_memory=True ) Delegation / subagents tools/delegate_tool.py:1559 Sync child (parent thread or pool) ThreadPoolExecutor(max_children) Yes (recursive) Independent per-child IterationBudget Partial (sandbox intersected with parent) Key insight. All five drivers are the same inner loop wrapped in different outer machinery — there is no longer any structurally distinct second loop (the RL/eval async loop was removed in the merge). Every driver converges on the shared tool surface — handle_function_call to registry.dispatch — so tool behavior is identical across the entire system regardless of which driver invoked it."},{"u":"architecture.html","p":"Architecture","t":"Surprising Design Choices","a":"surprises","x":"Surprising Design Choices Streaming is preferred always. Even when no display or TTS consumer is attached, the loop uses the streaming transport purely to gain stale-connection and read-timeout health checks the non-streaming path lacks ( agent/conversation_loop.py:958 ). The streamed response is repackaged into a non-streaming-shaped SimpleNamespace so the loop body is none the wiser. Tool-call JSON is re-serialized with sorted keys on every call. This keeps the prompt prefix byte-identical across turns for KV-cache reuse on local inference servers (llama.cpp/vLLM/Ollama) ( agent/conversation_loop.py:714 ). execute_code -only turns get an iteration refund. Programmatic \"RPC-style\" tool calling via the sandbox is treated as free so it does not eat the turn budget ( agent/conversation_loop.py:3773 ). Smart approval embeds a mini LLM guardian inside the gate. A side LLM call decides approve/deny/escalate before bothering the human ( tools/approval.py:990 ), inspired by OpenAI Codex's Smart Approvals ( tools/approval.py:996 ). Parallel file edits are path-aware. read_file / write_file / patch run concurrently only after a path-overlap check ( _paths_overlap compares path-prefix tuples), so disjoint subtrees parallelize while same-subtree edits serialize ( agent/tool_dispatch_helpers.py:166 ). Five recovery strategies guard every text exit. Partial-stream salvage, housekeeping-only"},{"u":"self-improvement.html","p":"Self-Improvement","t":"The Closed Learning Loop: How Hermes Adapts Over Time","a":"","x":"Most agents are stateless between sessions: every conversation starts from the same blank system prompt. Hermes is designed around the opposite premise — that the artifacts of one conversation should change the prompt of the next. This page traces each adaptation mechanism concretely: what fires it, what it reads and writes, where the result persists, and exactly how that persisted state re-enters a future prompt. Some of these form a genuinely closed loop today; others are real but human-in-the-loop, and a couple are honestly aspirational. We mark which is which."},{"u":"self-improvement.html","p":"Self-Improvement","t":"What a closed loop means here","a":"overview","x":"What a closed loop means here Every mechanism below is an instance of the same three-stage pattern. It is worth holding this template in mind, because the differences between the mechanisms are entirely in where each stage lives. Trigger What causes the adaptation to run — a turn counter crossing a threshold, a tool call, a compression event, or an explicit user command. Read / write What state the mechanism consults and what durable artifact it produces (a Markdown file, a SQLite row, a skill directory, a remote representation). Persistence Where the artifact lives across sessions, and crucially, whether it survives a process restart with no human involvement. Feedback The closing move: how the persisted artifact is re-injected into a future system prompt or recalled mid-conversation. Two architectural facts shape every mechanism. First, memory is read into the system prompt as a frozen snapshot at session start : mid-session writes hit disk immediately and durably, but the in-context copy is not rebuilt until the next session, which preserves the model provider's prefix cache for the whole conversation ( tools/memory_tool.py:11 ). Second, the heavier adaptation work (deciding what to remember, whether to write a skill) is deferred to a background fork that runs after the user's answer is delivered, so introspection never competes with the task for the model's attention ( agen"},{"u":"self-improvement.html","p":"Self-Improvement","t":"Curated memory and self-nudges","a":"curated-memory","x":"Curated memory and self-nudges The built-in memory provider is always present and cannot be removed; at most one external provider runs alongside it ( agent/memory_provider.py:1 , agent/memory_manager.py:253 ). It backs two bounded Markdown files in the profile's memories/ directory: File Holds Default bound MEMORY.md The agent's own notes — environment facts, project conventions, tool quirks, things learned 2200 chars USER.md What the agent knows about you — preferences, communication style, expectations, workflow habits 1375 chars The stores are deliberately bounded and curated rather than append-only logs ( tools/memory_tool.py:5 , tools/memory_tool.py:116 ). A write that would exceed the char limit is rejected with an explanatory error, forcing the model to consolidate rather than accumulate ( tools/memory_tool.py:328 ). Entries are separated by a section-sign delimiter and edited by short unique-substring matching — there are no IDs to track, so the model adds, replaces, removes, or reads by content ( tools/memory_tool.py:21 ). Character limits are used instead of token limits precisely because char counts are model-independent. Trigger: the periodic nudge Memory is not written only when the user says \"remember this.\" A turn counter ( _turns_since_memory ) increments every user turn; when it reaches the configurable nudge interval (default 10), a memory review is flagged f"},{"u":"self-improvement.html","p":"Self-Improvement","t":"The background review fork","a":"background-review","x":"The background review fork The nudge counters do not interrupt the conversation. Instead, after the response is delivered and only if the turn was not interrupted, Hermes spawns a daemon thread that forks a full second agent to do the introspection ( agent/turn_finalizer.py:393 , dispatched through the thin _spawn_background_review wrapper at run_agent.py:1419 ). The heavy lifting now lives in a dedicated module, agent/background_review.py . This is the engine that drives both curated-memory writes and autonomous skill creation, so it is worth understanding in detail. The fork is a complete AIAgent with the same model and provider, capped at 16 iterations, running in quiet mode with stdout/stderr redirected to /dev/null so it produces no visible output ( agent/background_review.py:402 ). It shares the parent's memory store by reference, and its own nudge intervals are zeroed so it cannot recurse ( agent/background_review.py:419 ). The parent's conversation snapshot is passed as conversation_history , one of three review prompts is run as the next user turn, and a per-thread tool whitelist restricts the fork to memory and skill tools so it can do nothing else ( agent/background_review.py:485 , agent/background_review.py:470 ). The prompts are concrete and skeptical. The memory prompt asks whether the user revealed persona, preferences, or behavioral expectations; the skill promp"},{"u":"self-improvement.html","p":"Self-Improvement","t":"Autonomous skill creation","a":"skill-creation","x":"Autonomous skill creation Skills are Hermes's procedural memory — narrow, actionable \"how to do this specific kind of task\" knowledge — as distinct from the broad declarative facts in MEMORY.md and USER.md ( tools/skill_manager_tool.py:10 ). A skill is a directory with a SKILL.md (YAML frontmatter plus a body) and optional references/ , templates/ , scripts/ , and assets/ subdirectories ( tools/skill_manager_tool.py:22 ). Trigger and write The skill-review branch of the background fork fires when _iters_since_skill crosses the skill nudge interval — that is, after a turn that took many tool iterations, which is a proxy for \"this was a hard, multi-step task\" ( agent/turn_finalizer.py:377 ). The fork is handed the skill review prompt and the conversation; if it judges the approach reusable, it calls skill_manage(action='create') to write a new skill into ~/.hermes/skills/ ( tools/skill_manager_tool.py:6 ). The prompt explicitly prefers patching a currently-loaded or existing class-level skill over creating a narrow new one, so the library trends toward umbrellas rather than a flat list ( agent/background_review.py:45 ). Caveat (and a default that changed). External hub installs are always security-scanned, but agent-created skills are scanned only when skills.guard_agent_created is turned on — and that flag is off by default , on the reasoning that the agent can already execute t"},{"u":"self-improvement.html","p":"Self-Improvement","t":"Skills that self-improve during use","a":"skill-self-improve","x":"Skills that self-improve during use The previous mechanism creates skills after the fact. This one improves them in the moment they are used , and it is one of the cleaner closed loops in the system. The skill index header that ships in every system prompt contains two standing instructions beyond \"load relevant skills\" ( agent/prompt_builder.py:1364 ): If a skill has issues, fix it with skill_manage(action='patch'). After difficult/iterative tasks, offer to save as a skill. If a skill you loaded was missing steps, had wrong commands, or needed pitfalls you discovered, update it before finishing. Because this guidance is in the prompt of the primary agent (not the background fork), the agent is directed to correct a skill while still inside the task that exposed the flaw. The patch action does a targeted find-and-replace inside SKILL.md or any supporting file, so the agent can surgically fix a wrong command or add a newly discovered pitfall without rewriting the whole skill ( tools/skill_manager_tool.py:17 ). The next agent that loads that skill reads the corrected version straight from disk — no re-derivation of the fix required. Key insight. This is the tightest loop in Hermes: the artifact being read (the skill) and the artifact being written (the patched skill) are the same file, and the write happens during the very task that revealed the defect. A wrong command discovered"},{"u":"self-improvement.html","p":"Self-Improvement","t":"FTS5 session search and cross-session recall","a":"session-recall","x":"FTS5 session search and cross-session recall Memory and skills capture distilled knowledge. Session search provides recall over the raw history: every past conversation transcript is stored in SQLite and made searchable through an FTS5 full-text index ( tools/session_search_tool.py:1 ). This is recall, not background learning — it fires when the model decides it needs it. Flow The session_search tool is a single shape with several calling modes inferred from the args, and — importantly since this page was first written — it now makes no LLM calls at all ; every shape returns actual messages straight from the DB ( tools/session_search_tool.py:23 ). With no args it browses recent sessions (titles, previews, timestamps). With a query it runs DISCOVERY: an FTS5 search, deduped by session lineage, returning the top N matching sessions ( tools/session_search_tool.py:393 ). With a session_id it either scrolls a window around an anchor message or reads the whole session by id. Several details make this robust: Anchored window plus bookends. Rather than dumping a whole transcript, each DISCOVERY hit returns a ±5-message window centered on the match (with the anchor flagged), plus the session's first three and last three user/assistant messages as bookends — enough to reconstruct goal → match → resolution without paying for the full transcript ( tools/session_search_tool.py:453 ). Lineag"},{"u":"self-improvement.html","p":"Self-Improvement","t":"SOUL, USER.md, and the insights model","a":"soul-insights","x":"SOUL, USER.md, and the insights model The model the agent holds of itself and of you spans three artifacts, each filling a different slot. SOUL.md The agent's identity, loaded as slot #1 of the system prompt when present ( agent/prompt_builder.py:1469 , wired through the load_soul_identity path at run_agent.py:476 ). On first run a default template is seeded into HERMES_HOME ( hermes_cli/default_soul.py:3 ). It is user-authored persona, not auto-learned — editing it changes who the agent is for every future session. USER.md The agent's model of you, written by the curated-memory loop described above and re-injected as the frozen snapshot on each session start. This is the auto-learned half of the self/user model. Insights An analytical model of your usage, not your identity. InsightsEngine queries the session database to produce token consumption, cost estimates, tool-usage patterns, and activity trends, surfaced via the /insights command ( agent/insights.py:84 , with the report assembled in agent/insights.py:102 ). Worth being precise here. SOUL.md and USER.md feed directly back into future prompts and are part of the closed loop. The insights engine is a reporting layer — it reads the database and renders a report for the human; nothing it computes is automatically written back into the agent's prompt. It informs your decisions about the agent, which is valuable, but it is no"},{"u":"self-improvement.html","p":"Self-Improvement","t":"Honcho dialectic user modeling","a":"honcho","x":"Honcho dialectic user modeling The optional Honcho plugin is the most sophisticated user-modeling backend, slotting in as the single allowed external memory provider ( plugins/memory/honcho/__init__.py:1 ). Where USER.md is a bounded local file, Honcho maintains an AI-native, server-side representation of each peer and reasons over it dialectically. It runs in one of three recall modes — context (auto-inject only), tools (model queries explicitly), or hybrid ( plugins/memory/honcho/__init__.py:308 ). Five tools, two layers of recall The plugin exposes honcho_profile (read/write a peer card), honcho_search (semantic excerpts, no synthesis), honcho_reasoning (synthesized dialectic answer at a chosen depth), honcho_context (raw session snapshot), and honcho_conclude (persist or delete a conclusion) ( plugins/memory/honcho/__init__.py:37 ). Recall assembles two layers each turn: a cached base context (user representation + peer card, refreshed on a configurable cadence) and a dialectic supplement produced by the reasoning engine ( plugins/memory/honcho/__init__.py:623 ). The dialectic itself \"Dialectic\" here is literal: _run_dialectic_depth runs up to three conditional .chat() passes (capped by the configurable dialecticDepth , default 1) against the user's representation ( plugins/memory/honcho/__init__.py:1030 ). Pass 0 is a cold-start or session-scoped query (\"Who is this person"},{"u":"self-improvement.html","p":"Self-Improvement","t":"The full learning loop","a":"loop-diagram","x":"The full learning loop The diagram traces one turn through the loop. The user's message is answered using context assembled from the frozen memory snapshot, the skill index, and recalled state. After the answer is delivered, nudge counters may spawn the background review fork, which writes durable artifacts. Those artifacts re-enter the system prompt at the start of the next session."},{"u":"self-improvement.html","p":"Self-Improvement","t":"Honest ledger: real loop vs aspiration","a":"honest-ledger","x":"Honest ledger: real loop vs aspiration Not every mechanism on this page closes the loop to the same degree. The table below states, per mechanism, whether the persisted output re-enters a future prompt automatically (a real closed loop), whether it assists a human-driven loop, or whether it remains aspirational. Mechanism Trigger Closes automatically? Notes Curated memory + nudge Turn counter (default 10) Yes Write to disk durable immediately; re-injected as frozen snapshot next session ( agent/turn_context.py:211 ). Pre-compression salvage Compression / reset Yes Provider on_pre_compress hook folds soon-to-be-lost context into the compression summary ( agent/memory_manager.py:686 ). Autonomous skill creation Tool-iteration counter Yes Background fork writes skill; appears in next session's skill index ( agent/turn_finalizer.py:377 ). Optionally security-scanned, off by default ( tools/skill_manager_tool.py:78 ). Skills self-improving in use Defect found while skill is loaded Yes Tightest loop: patch the same file mid-task; next load reads the fix ( agent/prompt_builder.py:1364 ). FTS5 session recall Model elects to search Partial Genuine cross-session recall, but pull-based — closes only when the model queries ( tools/session_search_tool.py:674 ). SOUL.md identity User edits the file Partial Feeds every future prompt, but human-authored, not auto-learned ( agent/prompt_builder"},{"u":"internals.html","p":"Internals","t":"Surprising Internals: Fresh Engineering in Hermes","a":"","x":"Hermes Agent is full of “huh, that’s clever” engineering where the non-obvious win is almost always one of three things: protecting the LLM’s context window, surviving real-world rate limits, or making stateless cloud backends behave like stateful shells. This page walks the standouts — what the mechanism is, and why it’s a genuinely good idea — starting with the headline feature, Programmatic Tool Calling, and working out through context compaction, credential rotation, six terminal backends, prompt caching, and the extensibility surface."},{"u":"internals.html","p":"Internals","t":"Programmatic Tool Calling: collapse N tool calls into one zero-context turn","a":"ptc","x":"Programmatic Tool Calling: collapse N tool calls into one zero-context turn The headline win. Instead of the model making ten separate tool calls — each round-tripping its full result back into the context window — the LLM writes a single Python script. That script imports an auto-generated hermes_tools.py stub module and calls real Hermes tools ( web_search , read_file , terminal , patch , …) as ordinary Python functions. The agent spends one inference turn; the script may make up to 50 tool calls internally, and only the script’s stdout returns to the model — intermediate tool results never enter the context window tools/code_execution_tool.py:8-29 . Surprise. The entire purpose of execute_code is that intermediate tool outputs never touch the LLM’s attention. A 50-call pipeline — search, fetch, parse, filter, reduce, loop — costs exactly one inference turn, because the filtering and looping happen in Python outside the context window. This is “programmatic tool calling” done right. One stub API, two interchangeable transports What makes this engineering-interesting rather than merely a sandbox is that the same generated stub API runs over two completely different transports, chosen by whether the terminal backend is local or remote tools/code_execution_tool.py:8-29 . Transport Mechanism Why Local (UDS) Parent opens an AF_UNIX socket, spawns the child, and runs an RPC listene"},{"u":"internals.html","p":"Internals","t":"Sandbox security: credential-stripped child env + head/tail output cap","a":"sandbox","x":"Sandbox security: credential-stripped child env + head/tail output cap The child process running LLM-authored code gets a deliberately minimal environment . _scrub_child_env walks os.environ and applies layered rules: (1) always pass skill-declared passthrough vars, (2) drop anything whose name contains KEY|TOKEN|SECRET|PASSWORD|CREDENTIAL|PASSWD|AUTH|DSN|WEBHOOK , and (3) only allow known-safe prefixes ( PATH , HOME , LANG , XDG_ , …) plus a short exact-name allow-list of operational HERMES_* vars tools/code_execution_tool.py:136-197 (the secret substrings and safe prefixes live at tools/code_execution_tool.py:87-91 ). The broad HERMES_ prefix was deliberately removed (#27303) so config-bearing HERMES_* vars no longer leak into the sandbox. Surprise. The script can call web_search and terminal , but it can never see the API keys that power them. The rationale is explicit in the code: “prevent credential exfiltration from LLM-generated scripts. The child accesses tools via RPC, not direct API.” The parent holds the keys and dispatches on the child’s behalf, so capability and credential are cleanly separated tools/code_execution_tool.py:1214-1223 . The env is otherwise hand-curated: it injects only TZ (popping the internal HERMES_TIMEZONE ) and can redirect HOME for per-profile isolation tools/code_execution_tool.py:1255-1269 . A 50KB cap that protects the answer The stdout cap "},{"u":"internals.html","p":"Internals","t":"Live context compaction: hash dedup, informative summaries, tool-pair repair","a":"compaction","x":"Live context compaction: hash dedup, informative summaries, tool-pair repair ContextCompressor is far smarter than the typical “summarize the middle” compactor. Before any LLM call, _prune_old_tool_results runs three cheap, structural passes that cost no inference at all agent/context_compressor.py:811-977 . Pass 1 — content-hash dedup Walking backward, it MD5-hashes each tool result over 200 chars. The first (newest) occurrence is kept in full; older identical results are replaced with a back-reference , [Duplicate tool output — same content as a more recent call] agent/context_compressor.py:889-913 . Surprise. Reading the same file five times used to cost five full copies in context. After dedup it costs one full copy plus four one-line back-references — identified purely by content hash, so it catches duplicates even when the tool calls look different. Pass 2 — information-bearing summaries, not placeholders Old tool outputs become a typed one-liner that carries information , for example [terminal] ran `npm test` -> exit 0, 47 lines output or [search_files] content search for 'compress' in agent/ -> 12 matches . _summarize_tool_result has bespoke formatting for roughly 20 tool types and even regex-extracts the exit code or match count out of the raw result agent/context_compressor.py:441-561 . The author explicitly contrasts this with “a generic placeholder that carries zero"},{"u":"internals.html","p":"Internals","t":"Credential pool: OAuth refresh, soft leases, status-aware cooldowns","a":"credentials","x":"Credential pool: OAuth refresh, soft leases, status-aware cooldowns CredentialPool is a production-grade multi-key rotator, not a round-robin list. Four details stand out. Status-aware cooldown TTLs A 429 (rate-limited) cools a key down for 1 hour ; other exhaustion uses a default TTL (a transient 401 cools only 5 minutes) agent/credential_pool.py:107-113 . Better still, it tries to compute an exact reset time from the provider: _normalize_error_context parses reset_at / resets_at / retry_until , and if those are absent, regex-extracts a retry after N seconds or quotaResetDelay from the error body, preferring that precise timestamp over the coarse TTL agent/credential_pool.py:288-333 . Cross-process token sync For Anthropic claude_code and OpenAI Codex entries, before declaring a key exhausted it re-reads the on-disk credentials file — picking up tokens that the Claude Code CLI or another Hermes profile refreshed in a separate process agent/credential_pool.py:541-616 . Surprise. The pool shares OAuth tokens across processes by re-reading the credentials file at the last moment. So if the Claude Code CLI silently refreshed a token a minute ago, Hermes picks it up instead of marking a perfectly good key as dead. That’s the kind of safeguard you only add after getting burned by a stale token. Soft leases for concurrency acquire_lease picks the least-leased available credential (pr"},{"u":"internals.html","p":"Internals","t":"Rate-limit handling: killing retry amplification + jittered backoff","a":"ratelimits","x":"Rate-limit handling: killing retry amplification + jittered backoff Two small files punch well above their weight. nous_rate_guard.py records a shared, atomically-written rate-limit state file so every session (CLI, gateway, cron, auxiliary) checks it before issuing a request. Surprise. The comment quantifies the exact bug it kills: “Each 429 from Nous triggers up to 9 API calls per conversation turn (3 SDK retries x 3 Hermes retries), and every one counts against RPH” agent/nous_rate_guard.py:1-11 . Recording the limit on the first 429 and short-circuiting the rest collapses a 9× amplification down to 1. It parses the reset time in a clear priority order — hourly RPH header → per-minute RPM header → generic retry-after agent/nous_rate_guard.py:39-67 — and writes the state file via mkstemp + an atomic rename for atomicity agent/nous_rate_guard.py:117-123 . retry_utils.jittered_backoff then replaces fixed exponential backoff with decorrelated jitter so multiple sessions hitting the same provider don’t all retry on the same tick. It seeds the RNG from time_ns() ^ (counter * 0x9E3779B9) — the golden-ratio constant — behind a lock, so even a coarse clock produces distinct jitter per call agent/retry_utils.py:19-57 . The companion rate_limit_tracker.py parses the full 12-header x-ratelimit-* schema into RPM/RPH/TPM/TPH buckets agent/rate_limit_tracker.py:8-20 , with elapsed-time-adj"},{"u":"internals.html","p":"Internals","t":"Six terminal backends + serverless hibernation","a":"backends","x":"Six terminal backends + serverless hibernation BaseEnvironment abstracts six backends — local, Docker, Singularity, SSH, Modal, Daytona — behind one execute() . The non-obvious cleverness is how it fakes a persistent login shell on top of spawn-per-call remote sandboxes. Faking a stateful shell Session snapshot Once at init init_session captures the login shell’s exported vars, functions, and aliases into a sourced file: export -p > snap; declare -f | grep ... >> snap; alias -p >> snap tools/environments/base.py:372-382 . Every later command sources that snapshot, then re-dumps env vars back into it (last-writer-wins), so an export FOO=bar in one call is visible in the next tools/environments/base.py:452-454 . CWD via stdout markers Since each remote command is a fresh process, CWD can’t persist naturally. _wrap_command appends printf '\\nMARKER%sMARKER\\n' \"$(pwd -P)\" ; the parent parses the marker out of stdout to track the working directory across calls tools/environments/base.py:417-467 . Surprise. Remote shells where each command is a brand-new process are made to behave like one persistent login shell — using a sourced env/function/alias snapshot plus in-band CWD markers smuggled through stdout. No daemon, no persistent connection required. Serverless hibernation: snapshot on cleanup, restore next session Cloud sandboxes auto-stop, so persistence is implemented as snapshot/"},{"u":"internals.html","p":"Internals","t":"Anthropic prompt caching: a rolling-window of breakpoints","a":"caching","x":"Anthropic prompt caching: a rolling-window of breakpoints A compact, correct implementation of the highest-leverage Anthropic cost optimization. apply_anthropic_cache_control places the 4 cache_control breakpoints (Anthropic’s maximum) as the system prompt — stable across every turn — plus the last 3 non-system messages as a rolling window agent/prompt_caching.py:49-79 . Key insight. The rolling tail means each new turn re-uses the cached prefix up to the previous breakpoint, so a multi-turn conversation gets roughly 75% input-token savings without ever managing cache state by hand. _apply_cache_marker carefully handles every content shape — tool messages (marked only when native Anthropic), empty content, raw strings (promoted to a text block), and content lists (mark the last block) — and supports the 1h TTL marker agent/prompt_caching.py:15-38 . It’s a pure function with no agent-state dependency, which makes it trivially testable."},{"u":"internals.html","p":"Internals","t":"Local-server fingerprinting","a":"routing","x":"Local-server fingerprinting Upstream change. The cheap-model “smart routing” downshift ( smart_model_routing.choose_cheap_model_route ) that this page used to describe was removed upstream (refactor #12732); the module no longer exists. What remains in this corner is the local-server fingerprinting below. model_metadata.detect_local_server_type fingerprints whatever local inference server is running by probing known endpoints in specificity order : LM Studio’s /api/v1/models first, then Ollama’s /api/tags (verifying the body actually contains models , because LM Studio returns 200 with an error body on that path), then llama.cpp’s /v1/props (with a /props fallback for old builds), then vLLM’s /version agent/model_metadata.py:478-536 . Graceful degradation. Each probe has a 2s timeout and swallows failures, so a misconfigured base_url degrades gracefully rather than hanging. The result feeds context-window detection, so a local model’s true context length is discovered rather than guessed."},{"u":"internals.html","p":"Internals","t":"Hooks, ACP adapter, and recursive toolset resolution","a":"extensibility","x":"Hooks, ACP adapter, and recursive toolset resolution Three smaller systems round out the extensibility surface. Filesystem-discovered hooks Drop a directory with HOOK.yaml + handler.py into ~/.hermes/hooks/ , declare lifecycle events, and the registry dynamically loads the module and registers handle() per event. The emit path supports wildcard fan-out ( command:* fires for any command:… ) and runs both sync and coroutine handlers, with every handler wrapped in try/except so “errors in hooks … never block the main pipeline” gateway/hooks.py:162-198 . A _register_builtin_hooks always-on extension point still exists but currently ships no built-in hooks — the former boot_md built-in is gone gateway/hooks.py:72-79 . ACP adapter renders real diffs The ACP adapter lets editors like Zed drive Hermes over the Agent Client Protocol. It maps every Hermes tool to an ACP ToolKind ( read / edit / execute / fetch / think ) acp_adapter/tools.py:21-56 and renders rich tool events: write_file and patch emit a proper diff content block via acp.tool_diff_content(old_text, new_text) so the editor shows a real inline diff, and terminal renders as $ command acp_adapter/tools.py:1029-1067 . It also extracts file locations (path + line) from tool args so the editor can jump to them acp_adapter/tools.py:1282-1291 . Cycle-safe toolset resolution Toolset resolution is recursive with composition ( includ"},{"u":"providers-auth.html","p":"Providers & Auth","t":"Models, Providers & Authentication","a":"","x":"Hermes routes inference through a single PROVIDER_REGISTRY of ~34 providers ( hermes_cli/auth.py:167-442 ), each tagged with one of six auth types. Three of the headline questions users ask — \"can I use my Claude Code / ChatGPT / Grok subscription instead of an API key?\" — all resolve here. The short answers: Anthropic subscription OAuth works mechanically but violates Anthropic's ToS ; an OpenAI ChatGPT subscription is a first-class, OpenAI-endorsed path for Codex models; and xAI/Grok now also ships a SuperGrok / Premium+ OAuth path ( xai-oauth ) alongside the plain API-key provider. This page maps the whole registry, then walks each provider's auth contract with verified verdicts."},{"u":"providers-auth.html","p":"Providers & Auth","t":"PROVIDER_REGISTRY — full map","a":"registry","x":"PROVIDER_REGISTRY — full map PROVIDER_REGISTRY is a Dict[str, ProviderConfig] defined at hermes_cli/auth.py:167 . Each ProviderConfig ( hermes_cli/auth.py:150-164 ) carries an id , name , auth_type , portal_base_url , inference_base_url , client_id , scope , an extra dict, api_key_env_vars (a priority-ordered tuple), and a base_url_env_var override. The registry is also auto-extended at import time with any api-key provider declared under providers/ ( hermes_cli/auth.py:444-474 ). Surprising design. The dataclass header comment lists four auth types ( hermes_cli/auth.py:155 : \"oauth_device_code\", \"oauth_external\", \"oauth_minimax\", or \"api_key\" ), but the registry actually uses six : oauth_device_code , oauth_external , oauth_minimax (MiniMax), api_key , external_process (Copilot ACP), and aws_sdk (Bedrock). And two providers registered as api_key — Anthropic and Copilot — secretly carry full OAuth flows. id name auth_type inference base URL key env vars / OAuth ids nous Nous Portal oauth_device_code inference-api.nousresearch.com/v1 client_id hermes-cli , scope inference:invoke , portal portal.nousresearch.com openai-codex OpenAI Codex oauth_external chatgpt.com/backend-api/codex client_id app_EMoamEEZ73f0CkXaXp7hrann , token url auth.openai.com/oauth/token qwen-oauth Qwen OAuth oauth_external portal.qwen.ai/v1 client_id f0304373b74a44d2b584a3fb70ca9e56 , token url chat.qwen.ai"},{"u":"providers-auth.html","p":"Providers & Auth","t":"Anthropic — OAuth, CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN & Claude Code spoofing","a":"anthropic","x":"Anthropic — OAuth, CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN & Claude Code spoofing Partial Registered api_key , but Anthropic is the most elaborate auth path in Hermes. It accepts a regular Console key and a Claude Pro/Max subscription OAuth token, and it even runs its own native PKCE login. Auth type api_key (with full hidden OAuth machinery) API key path sk-ant-api… Console key → x-api-key header (clean, supported) Subscription path CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN / ANTHROPIC_TOKEN / ~/.claude/.credentials.json / Hermes-native login → Authorization: Bearer with Claude Code identity spoofing Env var order ANTHROPIC_API_KEY → ANTHROPIC_TOKEN → CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN ( hermes_cli/auth.py:314 ) How the token shape decides the header get_anthropic_key() ( hermes_cli/auth.py:483-498 ) walks the env-var tuple in order. At runtime the agent calls resolve_anthropic_token() ( agent/anthropic_adapter.py:1159-1200 ), priority: (1) ANTHROPIC_TOKEN , (2) CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN , (3) the Claude Code credentials — macOS Keychain or ~/.claude/.credentials.json ( agent/anthropic_adapter.py:914-951 ), refreshed on expiry via _resolve_claude_code_token_from_credentials() ( agent/anthropic_adapter.py:1122-1134 ), (4) ANTHROPIC_API_KEY . The token shape then chooses the header: _is_oauth_token() ( agent/anthropic_adapter.py:386-411 ) returns True for sk-ant- (but NOT sk-ant-api ), eyJ… JWTs, and cc- Claude Code tokens. "},{"u":"providers-auth.html","p":"Providers & Auth","t":"OpenAI Codex — ChatGPT subscription OAuth","a":"codex","x":"OpenAI Codex — ChatGPT subscription OAuth Yes You sign in with a ChatGPT subscription (Plus/Pro/Team/Business/Enterprise/Edu) to drive Codex models — entirely separate from any OpenAI API key. The access token is the credential; there is no API-key path for Codex models in Hermes. Auth type oauth_external ( hermes_cli/auth.py:177-182 ) Base URL chatgpt.com/backend-api/codex — the same subscription-billed endpoint the official Codex CLI uses ( hermes_cli/auth.py:80 ) OAuth client client_id app_EMoamEEZ73f0CkXaXp7hrann ( hermes_cli/auth.py:96 ), token url auth.openai.com/oauth/token ( hermes_cli/auth.py:97 ) Billing Counts against your ChatGPT plan's included Codex limits — not metered API billing Device-code + PKCE login _login_openai_codex() ( hermes_cli/auth.py:6438-6511 ) first tries Hermes-stored creds, then optionally imports the Codex CLI's ~/.codex/auth.json via _import_codex_cli_tokens() ( hermes_cli/auth.py:3676-3707 ), else runs _codex_device_code_login() ( hermes_cli/auth.py:6925-7069 ): POST to auth.openai.com/api/accounts/deviceauth/usercode , show the user auth.openai.com/codex/device + a user code, poll for the authorization code, then PKCE-exchange it at auth.openai.com/oauth/token . Independent session — no write-back to the Codex CLI Hermes stores its own Codex session in ~/.hermes/auth.json under provider openai-codex with auth_mode=\"chatgpt\" via _save_codex_t"},{"u":"providers-auth.html","p":"Providers & Auth","t":"xAI / Grok — API key, plus new SuperGrok / Premium+ OAuth","a":"xai","x":"xAI / Grok — API key, plus new SuperGrok / Premium+ OAuth Two paths now The plain xai provider is still an API-key backend, but the upstream merge added a separate xai-oauth provider that authenticates a SuperGrok / Premium+ subscription — so the earlier \"API-key only, no subscription path\" framing no longer holds. Auth type ( xai ) api_key ( hermes_cli/auth.py:349-356 ) Auth type ( xai-oauth ) oauth_external ( hermes_cli/auth.py:191-196 ), base api.x.ai/v1 ( hermes_cli/auth.py:81 ) Base URL api.x.ai/v1 , override XAI_BASE_URL Env var XAI_API_KEY (the sole env var for the api-key path) Console console.x.ai — the pay-per-token developer API console ( hermes_cli/config.py:2603 ) Default models grok-4.3 (headline), grok-4.20-0309-reasoning , grok-4.20-0309-non-reasoning , grok-4.20-multi-agent-0309 ( hermes_cli/models.py:110-118 ) The plain xai provider has no login dispatcher — xai resolves through the generic api-key resolver. But xai-oauth now does ship a login ( _login_xai_oauth , hermes_cli/auth.py:6512 ) and runtime resolver ( resolve_xai_oauth_runtime_credentials ), and tools/xai_http.py grew beyond a bare User-Agent helper: alongside hermes_xai_user_agent() ( tools/xai_http.py:66-72 ) it now carries resolve_xai_http_credentials() ( tools/xai_http.py:75 ) that prefers Hermes-managed xAI OAuth creds before falling back to XAI_API_KEY . Aliases grok , x-ai , x.ai normalize to"},{"u":"providers-auth.html","p":"Providers & Auth","t":"GitHub Copilot & Copilot-ACP","a":"copilot","x":"GitHub Copilot & Copilot-ACP Two distinct providers, two very different auth models. copilot — hidden GitHub device-code OAuth api_key, but OAuth under the hood Registered auth_type=\"api_key\" ( hermes_cli/auth.py:217-224 ), yet it ships a real GitHub device-code flow. The supported prefixes are gho_ (OAuth), github_pat_ (fine-grained PAT with Copilot Requests permission), and ghu_ (GitHub App) ( hermes_cli/copilot_auth.py:36 ); validate_copilot_token ( hermes_cli/copilot_auth.py:46-64 ) explicitly rejects classic ghp_ PATs. resolve_copilot_token() ( hermes_cli/copilot_auth.py:67-95 ) checks COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN → GH_TOKEN → GITHUB_TOKEN ( hermes_cli/copilot_auth.py:39 ), then falls back to gh auth token ( _try_gh_cli_token , hermes_cli/copilot_auth.py:119-150 ). The interactive login copilot_device_code_login() ( hermes_cli/copilot_auth.py:155-290 ) runs the GitHub OAuth device flow with client_id Ov23li8tweQw6odWQebz (the opencode/Copilot-CLI app id, hermes_cli/copilot_auth.py:33 ), scope read:user , polling github.com/login/oauth/access_token ; the resulting token is saved via save_env_value(\"COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN\", token) ( hermes_cli/model_setup_flows.py:1539-1541 ). Requests carry editor-spoofing headers ( Editor-Version: vscode/1.104.1 , Copilot-Integration-Id: vscode-chat , hermes_cli/copilot_auth.py:383-385 ). The secret resolver special-cases copilot ( hermes_cli/auth.p"},{"u":"providers-auth.html","p":"Providers & Auth","t":"Qwen-OAuth, Gemini & Nous Portal","a":"qwen-gemini-nous","x":"Qwen-OAuth, Gemini & Nous Portal Qwen-OAuth — parasitic on the Qwen CLI oauth_external Base portal.qwen.ai/v1 ( hermes_cli/auth.py:197-202 ). Crucially, Hermes does not run its own Qwen device flow — it reads and refreshes the Qwen CLI's credential file ~/.qwen/oauth_creds.json via _read_qwen_cli_tokens() ( hermes_cli/auth.py:1937-1947 , path at hermes_cli/auth.py:1933-1934 ). If missing it errors \"Run 'qwen auth qwen-oauth' first.\" Refresh ( _refresh_qwen_cli_tokens , hermes_cli/auth.py:2001-2071 ) POSTs to chat.qwen.ai/api/v1/oauth2/token ( hermes_cli/auth.py:108 ). resolve_qwen_runtime_credentials() ( hermes_cli/auth.py:2092-2121 ) returns source=\"qwen-cli\" ; the dispatcher then tags it api_mode=\"chat_completions\" ( hermes_cli/runtime_provider.py:1451-1462 ). Surprising design. Qwen \"OAuth\" mints no tokens of its own — it is effectively a parasitic session on the official Qwen CLI login. It is a subscription/OAuth provider, but only because the Qwen CLI did the logging-in. Gemini — pure API key api_key Base generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta , env GOOGLE_API_KEY / GEMINI_API_KEY ( hermes_cli/auth.py:232-239 ) — a Google AI Studio API key. Note: the merge also added a separate OAuth provider google-gemini-cli ( hermes_cli/auth.py:203-208 ) for the Gemini CLI's Cloud Code path, so \"no Google subscription login at all\" no longer holds for the broader registry. Nous Portal"},{"u":"providers-auth.html","p":"Providers & Auth","t":"Auth storage & runtime resolution","a":"storage","x":"Auth storage & runtime resolution All Hermes-owned OAuth/credential state lives in ~/.hermes/auth.json ( hermes_cli/auth.py:855-856 ), guarded by a reentrant cross-process advisory lock _auth_store_lock() ( hermes_cli/auth.py:1023-1039 ) backed by _file_lock (fcntl/msvcrt, hermes_cli/auth.py:951-1021 ) with AUTH_LOCK_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=15 at hermes_cli/auth.py:67 . The one exception is Anthropic's native OAuth, stored separately in ~/.hermes/.anthropic_oauth.json ( agent/anthropic_adapter.py:1254 ). Per-provider OAuth tokens are additionally fanned out into a cross-process credential pool ( agent/credential_pool.py ) that the api-key and Codex/Nous resolvers fall back to. File Owner Hermes access ~/.hermes/auth.json Hermes read/write — all provider OAuth state ~/.hermes/.anthropic_oauth.json Hermes read/write — native Anthropic PKCE creds ~/.claude/.credentials.json Claude Code CLI read + refresh write-back (scopes merge) ~/.codex/auth.json Codex CLI import only (no write-back) ~/.qwen/oauth_creds.json Qwen CLI read + refresh write-back Runtime dispatch resolve_runtime_provider() ( hermes_cli/runtime_provider.py:1254-1714 ) is the central dispatcher. After custom/Azure short-circuits and a credential-pool check, it branches per provider: Nous→invoke-JWT resolve ( hermes_cli/runtime_provider.py:1391 ); Codex→ codex_responses ( hermes_cli/runtime_provider.py:1413 ); xai-oauth→ codex"},{"u":"faq.html","p":"FAQ","t":"Research Answers & FAQ","a":"","x":"Three headline questions drove this research: can you point Hermes at your Anthropic Claude Code Max/20x, OpenAI ChatGPT/Codex, or xAI Grok subscription instead of paying per-token for an API key? The short version is that all three are technically wired into Hermes, but only one of them is something the provider actually permits. Below, each headline question gets a verdict badge, a crisp answer, a concrete “how to,” and a Terms-of-Service caveat — followed by a broader FAQ on Copilot, Qwen/Gemini, Nous, how to escape per-token billing, the legal/risk picture, and the single safest path."},{"u":"faq.html","p":"FAQ","t":"Q1 — Anthropic Claude Code (Pro / Max 5x / Max 20x) subscription vs API key","a":"headline-anthropic","x":"Q1 — Anthropic Claude Code (Pro / Max 5x / Max 20x) subscription vs API key Partial — works, but violates Anthropic ToS Technically yes, policy-wise no. Hermes has a fully built-out path to drive a Claude Pro/Max subscription: it accepts CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN / claude setup-token output / the Claude Code credential file, ships its own native PKCE login (“Authorize Hermes with your Claude Pro/Max subscription”), and will happily make subscription-backed inference calls. But it only works by impersonating Anthropic’s first-party Claude Code client. Per Anthropic’s own Legal & Compliance docs, OAuth/subscription credentials are “intended exclusively” for native Anthropic applications, and Anthropic began technically blocking and suspending exactly these spoofing harnesses starting January 2026. The only unambiguously legitimate Anthropic option is a pay-as-you-go Console API key ( sk-ant-api… ). Key insight. “Hermes accepts the credential” and “Anthropic permits the usage” are two different statements. Hermes’ acceptance is implemented through first-party-client impersonation that Anthropic explicitly disallows and is actively enforcing against — so the credential works while the usage is forbidden. How to Legitimate (recommended) — Console pay-as-you-go API key. Get an sk-ant-api… key from console.anthropic.com, then export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-api... (or put it in Hermes’ "},{"u":"faq.html","p":"FAQ","t":"Q2 — OpenAI ChatGPT / Codex subscription vs API key","a":"headline-openai","x":"Q2 — OpenAI ChatGPT / Codex subscription vs API key Yes — subscription is the primary supported path Yes — you can drive OpenAI Codex models in Hermes with just your ChatGPT subscription (Plus/Pro/Team/Business/Enterprise/Edu), with no OpenAI API key . Hermes ships a first-class openai-codex provider (auth_type oauth_external ) that runs OpenAI’s own “Sign in with ChatGPT” device-code + PKCE flow and routes inference through https://chatgpt.com/backend-api/codex — the same subscription-billed endpoint the official Codex CLI uses. The access token is the credential; there is no API-key path for Codex models in Hermes at all. This is OpenAI-endorsed for personal use (Romain Huet, OpenAI, ~March 2026: “use Codex and their ChatGPT subscription wherever they like”), and the Codex CLI is Apache-2.0 with permissive ToS. Key insight. For OpenAI’s own models, the subscription route is the primary supported path in Hermes, not an afterthought. There is no generic openai API-key provider in the registry — pay-per-token access to OpenAI models is only reachable indirectly via OpenRouter or Vercel AI Gateway. How to Option A — let Hermes run the OAuth (no API key, no Codex CLI needed). Run hermes auth (or hermes login ) and pick “OpenAI Codex.” This triggers _codex_device_code_login() ( hermes_cli/auth.py:6925 ), which POSTs to https://auth.openai.com/api/accounts/deviceauth/usercode with c"},{"u":"faq.html","p":"FAQ","t":"Q3 — xAI Grok CLI / subscription vs API key","a":"headline-xai","x":"Q3 — xAI Grok CLI / subscription vs API key Integrated Subscription OAuth now exists (post-merge) This is the answer the upstream merge changed most. xAI/Grok is still available as a plain API-key provider against api.x.ai/v1 , but the merged tree also adds a real xAI OAuth provider , xai-oauth (registry name “xAI Grok OAuth (SuperGrok / Premium+)”, hermes_cli/auth.py:191-196 ). It runs a genuine browser-loopback PKCE flow — _xai_oauth_loopback_login() ( hermes_cli/auth.py:6721 ), issuer https://auth.x.ai , client_id b1a00492-073a-47ea-816f-4c329264a828 , scope openid profile email offline_access grok-cli:access api:access ( hermes_cli/auth.py:99-105 ) — and at runtime resolve_xai_oauth_runtime_credentials() ( hermes_cli/auth.py:4189 ) uses the refreshed OAuth access token as the credential. So a SuperGrok / Premium+ subscription can now drive Grok in Hermes via OAuth, not only via a pay-per-token key. The plain API-key path is unchanged: in the registry, xai is auth_type=\"api_key\" , base https://api.x.ai/v1 , env XAI_API_KEY , override XAI_BASE_URL ( hermes_cli/auth.py:349-356 , verified directly). Key insight (revised). The pre-merge fork had no xAI subscription path at all — API key only. The merge added a first-class xai-oauth provider that authenticates a SuperGrok / Premium+ subscription through xAI’s own grok-cli:access -scoped OAuth, alongside the still-present plain XA"},{"u":"faq.html","p":"FAQ","t":"GitHub Copilot subscription","a":"copilot","x":"GitHub Copilot subscription Yes — subscription works via GitHub device-code OAuth Despite being registered as auth_type=\"api_key\" ( hermes_cli/auth.py:217-224 ), the copilot provider ships a real GitHub device-code OAuth flow, so a GitHub Copilot subscription works as a model backend. copilot_device_code_login() ( hermes_cli/copilot_auth.py:155-275 ) runs the GitHub OAuth device flow with client_id Ov23li8tweQw6odWQebz (the same one used by opencode and the Copilot CLI), scope read:user , and returns the resulting gho_ token (the caller persists it as COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN ). Token resolution checks COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN → GH_TOKEN → GITHUB_TOKEN then falls back to gh auth token ( resolve_copilot_token() , hermes_cli/copilot_auth.py:67-96 ). Supported token prefixes are gho_ (OAuth), github_pat_ (fine-grained PAT with Copilot Requests permission), and ghu_ — classic ghp_ PATs are rejected ( hermes_cli/copilot_auth.py:35-36 ). A second variant, copilot-acp ( auth_type=\"external_process\" , base acp://copilot , hermes_cli/auth.py:225-231 ), holds no token at all — it shells out to the Copilot CLI ( copilot --acp --stdio , args resolved at agent/copilot_acp_client.py:64-67 ) via subprocess.Popen in CopilotACPClient._run_prompt ( agent/copilot_acp_client.py:438-462 ) and delegates auth entirely to whatever that process is logged into. Caveat. Copilot requests carry editor-spoofing hea"},{"u":"faq.html","p":"FAQ","t":"Qwen & Gemini OAuth","a":"qwen-gemini","x":"Qwen & Gemini OAuth Qwen — subscription via Qwen CLI session Gemini — API key only Qwen-OAuth Qwen is a real subscription/OAuth provider ( oauth_external , base https://portal.qwen.ai/v1 , hermes_cli/auth.py:197-202 ), but it does not mint its own tokens . Instead it reads and refreshes the Qwen CLI’s credential file ~/.qwen/oauth_creds.json ( hermes_cli/auth.py:1933-1959 ); if it is missing, Hermes errors with “Run ‘qwen auth qwen-oauth’ first” ( hermes_cli/auth.py:1941 ). Refresh POSTs grant_type=refresh_token with client_id f0304373b74a44d2b584a3fb70ca9e56 to https://chat.qwen.ai/api/v1/oauth2/token and writes the rotated pair back to the Qwen CLI file ( _refresh_qwen_cli_tokens() , hermes_cli/auth.py:2001-2070 ). So Qwen piggybacks on an existing Qwen CLI login rather than running its own device flow. Gemini The plain gemini provider is api_key — base https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta , env GOOGLE_API_KEY / GEMINI_API_KEY ( hermes_cli/auth.py:232-239 ); a Google AI Studio API key is its only credential. (The merge also added a separate google-gemini-cli OAuth provider, hermes_cli/auth.py:203-208 , that reuses a Gemini-CLI login — but the plain key path above is the simplest.)"},{"u":"faq.html","p":"FAQ","t":"Nous Portal subscription","a":"nous","x":"Nous Portal subscription Yes — the canonical first-party subscription provider Nous is the one provider where subscription auth is fully native and uncontested. It is registered as oauth_device_code ( hermes_cli/auth.py:168-176 ) with client_id hermes-cli , scope inference:invoke (the NOUS_INFERENCE_INVOKE_SCOPE constant, hermes_cli/auth.py:73 ), and portal https://portal.nousresearch.com . The device-code login ( _nous_device_code_login() , hermes_cli/auth.py:7502 ) opens the portal verification URL. The minting-via-separate-POST model the pre-merge fork used is gone : the merged tree instead treats the OAuth access token itself as an inference:invoke -scoped JWT and uses it directly as the inference credential. At runtime resolve_nous_runtime_credentials() ( hermes_cli/auth.py:5268 ) refreshes that JWT, validates its inference:invoke scope, and returns it with source=\"invoke_jwt\" ; _set_nous_agent_key_from_invoke_jwt() ( hermes_cli/auth.py:1855 ) derives the TTL straight from the JWT’s exp claim and reuses the token until it nears expiry. That invoke JWT is what hits inference-api.nousresearch.com/v1 . A separate nous_subscription.py layer maps the Nous subscription to managed tool features — web search, image generation, TTS (including the “xAI TTS” label at hermes_cli/nous_subscription.py:212 ), browser, and modal execution — gated on Nous auth status ( get_nous_subscriptio"},{"u":"faq.html","p":"FAQ","t":"Which providers let me avoid per-token API billing?","a":"avoid-per-token","x":"Which providers let me avoid per-token API billing? Several providers in Hermes can be funded by a subscription rather than metered API tokens — Nous, OpenAI Codex, GitHub Copilot, Qwen-OAuth, and (added by the upstream merge) xAI xai-oauth . Only some of them are clean. Provider Subscription path Mechanism Standing Nous Portal Yes Native oauth_device_code → inference:invoke JWT used directly First-party, fully sanctioned OpenAI Codex Yes “Sign in with ChatGPT” OAuth → backend-api/codex Endorsed for personal use; pooling/reselling forbidden GitHub Copilot Yes GitHub device-code OAuth ( gho_ token) Works; uses editor-spoofing headers Qwen-OAuth Yes Reuses the Qwen CLI’s OAuth session Piggybacks on an existing CLI login xAI / Grok (SuperGrok) Yes xai-oauth — xAI’s own grok-cli:access OAuth (post-merge) Sanctioned xAI CLI-agent auth; single-user Anthropic Claude ToS OAuth/setup-token + Claude Code impersonation Forbidden by Anthropic; actively enforced xAI / Grok (API key) No Plain api_key ( console.x.ai ) Per-token API billing Gemini, DeepSeek, Z.AI, Kimi, MiniMax, … No Plain api_key Per-token API billing Everything else in the PROVIDER_REGISTRY ( hermes_cli/auth.py:167-442 , ~34 statically-declared entries plus any auto-registered from providers/ ) is plain API-key billing. OpenRouter and Vercel AI Gateway are aggregator API keys that bill per-token but consolidate many models b"},{"u":"faq.html","p":"FAQ","t":"Is any of this against ToS / risky?","a":"is-it-risky","x":"Is any of this against ToS / risky? Yes for two of the providers, and the risk is concrete, not hypothetical. Anthropic (high risk) Routing a Pro/Max subscription through Hermes violates the Consumer Terms of Service. Anthropic has blocked subscription-token support in OpenCode, Roo, Goose, Cursor, Manus, and Pi since January 2026, and reportedly suspended accounts “without prior notice.” The path only functions via first-party impersonation, which the code itself describes as dodging Anthropic’s content filters ( agent/anthropic_adapter.py:2338-2347 ). Real risk of token revocation or account ban. OpenAI Codex (conditional) Honest single-user reuse of your own ChatGPT subscription is endorsed. Multi-account pooling, shared deployment, or reselling is a clear ToS violation and ban risk. The undocumented backend endpoint can also change or gate non-official clients at any time. GitHub Copilot (low–moderate) Works through GitHub’s device-code OAuth, but presents editor-spoofing identity headers. Treat GitHub’s acceptable-use terms for Copilot as binding; personal single-user use is the safe zone. xAI / Grok (low) The merge added a real xai-oauth subscription path, but it authenticates through xAI’s own grok-cli:access OAuth client rather than by spoofing a chat session — so honest single-user reuse of your SuperGrok / Premium+ subscription sits in xAI’s intended zone. The risk is"},{"u":"faq.html","p":"FAQ","t":"What’s the safest, fully-supported path?","a":"safest-path","x":"What’s the safest, fully-supported path? If your priority is “nothing that risks an account suspension or relies on impersonation,” here is the ranked guidance. Nous Portal subscription — the cleanest subscription option. Native OAuth, first-party client, the access token is the short-lived inference JWT. No ToS gray area ( hermes_cli/auth.py:168-176 ). Anthropic Console API key ( sk-ant-api… ) — the only legitimate way to use Claude models in Hermes. Routed via x-api-key with no spoofing ( agent/anthropic_adapter.py:811-815 ), billed per-token under the Commercial Terms. OpenAI Codex via your own ChatGPT subscription — fully supported in Hermes and endorsed by OpenAI for personal single-user use . Stay single-user; do not pool or share. xAI Grok via your own SuperGrok / Premium+ subscription — the new xai-oauth path uses xAI’s own grok-cli:access OAuth, so single-user reuse is in the sanctioned zone. Any plain API-key provider (Gemini, xAI, DeepSeek, Kimi, Z.AI, MiniMax, OpenRouter, AI Gateway, …) — ordinary metered billing, no policy entanglement. The one thing to avoid. Do not drive an Anthropic Claude Pro/Max subscription through Hermes on an account you care about. It works mechanically, but it depends on impersonating Claude Code, violates Anthropic’s Consumer Terms, and is the exact pattern Anthropic has been blocking and suspending since January 2026. For Anthropic mode"}];
